<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pulakeshin I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 543–566</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Pulakesi I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pulakeshin I was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In 543 he fortified Vatapi, modern Badami, and performed the horse sacrifice to proclaim his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, freeing his family from the overlordship of the Kadambas. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> made the Chalukyas the leading power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pulakeshin was the son and successor of Ranaraga and a grandson of Jayasimha, the earliest historically attested ruler of the family. Records of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Kalyani Chalukyas</a> name his father as Vijayaditya, but historians set these aside, since the Vatapi Chalukya records name Ranaraga explicitly. The name appears in inscriptions as Polekeshin, Polikeshin and Pulikeshin; J. F. Fleet and D. C. Sircar read it as a Sanskrit-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> hybrid meaning tiger-haired, while K. A. Nilakanta Sastri derived it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> words for great and lion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As the first sovereign ruler of his line, he has been called the real founder of the dynasty. His earliest inscription, issued at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> under the title Vallabheshvara, records the Ashvamedha, but the early records give no details of his campaigns, and Sircar suggested that the dynasty's military successes in this period belonged to his son and commander-in-chief Kirttivarman I. Inscriptions give him titles such as Satyashraya, Rana-vikrama, Shri-prithvi-vallabha, which marked paramountcy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a>-maharaja. A stone tablet at Amminabhavi of 566-567 records his grant to the shrine of the deity Kalideva on the occasion of a solar eclipse.</p><p>The epigraphic evidence shows him following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-religion">Vedic religion</a>. The Mahakuta Pillar inscription of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalesha">Mangalesha</a> lists the Agnishtoma, Vajapeya, Ashvamedha, Hiranyagarbha and other Shrauta sacrifices, and the Nerur inscription credits him with knowledge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manusmriti">Manusmriti</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>. Nilakanta Sastri took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a>-maharaja to mean that he promoted the Vedic faith against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>He married Durlabha-devi of the Bappura lineage, whom the Mahakuta inscription compares to Damayanti; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a> inscription's mention of Indukanti most likely names another queen. He was succeeded first by Kirttivarman I, whose own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> inscription implies an accession in 566-567, and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalesha">Mangalesha</a>. A prince named Pugavarman is sometimes taken for another son, though he may have been Mangalesha's.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pulakeshin I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The founder of the dynasty’s power was <b>Pulakeshin I</b>, who, according to a rock inscription at Badami dated 543, fortified the hill of Vatapi, the modern town of Badami, and who performed the ashvamedha, or horse sacrifice, to proclaim his sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Weakened by divisions between branches, the Kadambas were conquered around 540 by <b>Pulakeshin I</b> of the Chalukyas of Badami.</blockquote><a href="/state/kadamba-dynasty">Kadamba dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
